ete slop all the way down. ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"start","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"start","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"The transformer architecture of an LLM inherently makes it such that you can never be “creative” by it alone. An AI bot is empty insofar as it just produces a pattern of some kind, but this in isolation isn’t that malicious. No, what’s more insidious is when there are still living and breathing humans that simply hit “publish” without any other engagement. This is worse than dead internet theory. How does that make you any different from said machine then? Doing this means you’ve given up and surrendered your ability to engage with the world in a meaningful way.","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"start","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"start","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"I’ve become so conditioned by encountering these things that I second guess myself now when I write. I genuinely will edit something I’ve written because I think it “sounds too AI”, and avoid the use of emdashes and hyphens for the same reason. If a paragraph is around 3 sentences long (this is how I was taught in university), I think the structure “looks like AI” as I’ve observed LLMs have a tendency of sticking with shorter paragraphs rather than long ones. ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"start","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"start","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Aristotle refers to a term called “lógos” which roughly translates to word, discourse or reason. That is, the written word means one’s ability to formulate their own thinking, understanding and order. This is all very philosophical, but it is also deeply rooted in human psychology insofar as our ability to write is a ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"693d3391a7bbb89525abc79d","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://summit.sfu.ca/item/2296","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"primary driver of our cognitive development","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":" (particularly in children).","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"start","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"start","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"LLMs are cool sure. I’ve used them a lot particularly this past year to brainstorm, debug and learn as a developer. But I would never want to voluntarily make myself an empty mouthpiece for an LLM. Doing so is the equivalent of unplugging my brain completely for some memetic machine that is far, far from actual intelligence. ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0}],"direction":null}}', 'Writing with AI is a cop out', NULL, 'It sounds like shit and it’s obvious', '2025-12-13T09:00:00.000Z', 0, 'writing-with-ai-is-a-cop-out', '2025-12-13T09:54:39.345Z', '2025-12-13T09:34:52.493Z', 'published', '2025-12-13T09:54:39.356Z', '2025-12-13T09:54:39.356Z', 0, 0);
