 about how they have certs from GoDaddy (lol) and for the user to use antivirus on their computer. This should have been a massive red flag to anyone with a modicum of computer security knowledge looking to procure their services.","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"The fact that MMH somehow became the preferred healthcare portal in New Zealand is insane to me. They haven''t apologised about the breach and instead are putting out the weakest damage control. This is clearly a massive fuck up on their part. From ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"695c5a9acaf42b7e1bbbd726","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7413120799634743296/","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"a recent LinkedIn post they state","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":":","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"\"The investigation has identified that one module, Health Documents, within the app was compromised, not the entire app. Manage My Health is commencing legal action to protect client data.\"","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":2,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":2},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Based on what I''ve read and inferred, this \"module\" was essentially a fuck up with how they set up Azure Blob storage where all documents and attachments were stored. I''m not going to post the exact source here, but some searches on Twitter will bring up posts that show this may well be the case. So this has likely been public for a very long time before Kazu even came across it.","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"I also found the following point from MMH very rich:","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"\"Manage My Health has [...] Re-secured all health documents and strengthened document storage controls.\"","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":2,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":2},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Bro, you cannot \"re-secure\" documents that were publicly accessible. They were not \"secure\" in the first place, so at most you are fixing access to the blob storage/bucket but the files themselves are wide open and fucked now. Also, nagging your users on making sure they have secure passwords and 2FA is meaningless if you can''t secure your own infra. ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"This is shaping up to be one of the worst cybersecurity breaches in New Zealand and so far it appears that no one will be having to face the music. It is a failure on MMH''s part, but it is a wider signal showing the poor state of the system itself and its shitty standards. ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"695c5b6dcaf42b7e1bbbd727","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://www.openaccess.nz/blog/managemyhealth-hack-a-failure-of-standards/","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Callum McMenamin has written an excellent account of this","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":" that goes over this in great detail. Self-attestation is a massive issue with vendors - we need a legal standard that companies need to follow to ensure the secure handling of user data, and if not then appropriate fines and/or jail.","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0}],"direction":null}}', 'People deserve better', NULL, 'Some thoughts on the recent Manage My Health hack', '2026-01-05T22:30:00.000Z', 0, 'people-deserve-better', '2026-01-06T00:54:41.883Z', '2026-01-06T00:12:09.564Z', 'published', '2026-01-06T00:54:41.904Z', '2026-01-06T00:54:41.904Z', 0, 0);
INSERT INTO "_posts_v" ("id", "parent_id", "version_title", "version_hero_image_id", "version_content", "version_meta_title", "version_meta_image_id", "version_meta_description", "version_published_at", "version_generate_slug", "version_slug", "version_updated_at", "version_created_at", "version__status", "created_at", "updated_at", "latest", "autosave") VALUES (87, 24, 'People deserve better', NULL, '{"root":{"type":"root","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Over the Christmas and New Year period here in New Zealand, one of the largest and widely used healthcare portals used by doctors and health centres ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"695c545acaf42b7e1bbbd723","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/583248/manage-my-health-data-breach-ransom-deadline-arrives","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"got hacked","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":". 108GiB worth of personal health data, including (in some cases) passport scans, prescription information, names, addresses, NHI numbers etc from this Manage My Health (MMH). Initially the group responsible (Kazu) asked for a 300K ransom, but then lowered this to 60K in an effort to try and force MMH''s hand.","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"block","fields":{"id":"695c54b2caf42b7e1bbbd724","media":26,"blockName":"","blockType":"mediaBlock"},"format":"","version":2},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"MMH has basically been completely silent since the hack was made public. Most health practices found out about the breach via media outlets, and only after repeated attempts to contact MMH failed so Kazu then posted a sample of the hacked data. It is unclear how the breach happened, if the exploit has since been fixed, who exactly has been affected and how long the vulnerability has existed in the MMH portal.","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"While the government is chasing it''s tail launching all sorts of different reviews and injunctions, much of this in my opinion is focusing on the wrong questions. It is unacceptable for a ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"mode":"normal","text":"HEALTHCARE","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":2,"version":1},{"mode":"normal","text":" SaaS platform to not have SOC 2 compliance and basic opsec at a minimum. They should be as secure as financial services like banks, if not more secure. Given how many health centres use MMH as a way for patients to manage prescription repeats, bookings etc, it is unfair for normal everyday people who have been pushed to use this shitty platform only to have their data mishandled.","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Their ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"695c5780caf42b7e1bbbd725","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://archive.is/6VGLs","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"security page is incredibly vague","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":" and just talks about SSL certificates. It doesn''t specify anything like where the data is stored, how it is stored (encrypted at rest, in transit, etc), if MMH undertake security audits, and instead just talks about how they have certs from GoDaddy (lol) and for the user to use antivirus on their computer. This should have been a massive red flag to anyone with a modicum of computer security knowledge looking to procure their services.","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"The fact that MMH somehow became the preferred healthcare portal in New Zealand is insane to me. They haven''t apologised about the breach and instead are putting out the weakest damage control. This is clearly a massive fuck up on their part. From ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"695c5a9acaf42b7e1bbbd726","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7413120799634743296/","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"a recent LinkedIn post they state","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":":","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"\"The investigation has identified that one module, Health Documents, within the app was compromised, not the entire app. Manage My Health is commencing legal action to protect client data.\"","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":2,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":2},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Based on what I''ve read and inferred, this \"module\" was essentially a fuck up with how they set up Azure Blob storage where all documents and attachments were stored. I''m not going to post the exact source here, but some searches on Twitter will bring up posts that show this may well be the case. So this has likely been public for a very long time before Kazu even came across it.","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"I also found the following point from MMH very rich:","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"\"Manage My Health has [...] Re-secured all health documents and strengthened document storage controls.\"","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":2,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":2},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Bro, you cannot \"re-secure\" documents that were publicly accessible. They were not \"secure\" in the first place, so at most you are fixing access to the blob storage/bucket but the files themselves are wide open and fucked now. Also, nagging your users on making sure they have secure passwords and 2FA is meaningless if you can''t secure your own infra. ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"This is shaping up to be one of the worst cybersecurity breaches in New Zealand and so far it appears that no one will be having to face the music. It is a failure on MMH''s part, but it is a wider signal showing the poor state of the system itself and its shitty standards. ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"695c5b6dcaf42b7e1bbbd727","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://www.openaccess.nz/blog/managemyhealth-hack-a-failure-of-standards/","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Callum McMenamin has written an excellent account of this","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":" that goes over this in great detail. Self-attestation is a massive issue with vendors - we need a legal standard that companies need to follow to ensure the secure handling of user data, and if not then appropriate fines and/or jail.","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0}],"direction":null}}', 'People deserve better', NULL, 'Some thoughts on the recent Manage My Health hack', '2026-01-05T22:30:00.000Z', 0, 'people-deserve-better', '2026-01-06T01:02:08.808Z', '2026-01-06T00:12:09.564Z', 'published', '2026-01-06T01:02:08.824Z', '2026-01-06T01:02:08.824Z', 1, 0);
INSERT INTO "_posts_v" ("id", "parent_id", "version_title", "version_hero_image_id", "version_content", "version_meta_title", "version_meta_image_id", "version_meta_description", "version_published_at", "version_generate_slug", "version_slug", "version_updated_at", "version_created_at", "version__status", "created_at", "updated_at", "latest", "autosave") VALUES (88, 25, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-01-11T23:00:01.756Z', '2026-01-11T23:00:01.755Z', 'draft', '2026-01-11T23:00:01.859Z', '2026-01-11T23:00:01.859Z', 0, 0);
INSERT INTO "_posts_v" ("id", "parent_id", "version_title", "version_hero_image_id", "version_content", "version_meta_title", "version_meta_image_id", "version_meta_description", "version_published_at", "version_generate_slug", "version_slug", "version_updated_at", "version_created_at", "version__status", "created_at", "updated_at", "latest", "autosave") VALUES (89, 25, 'Using agents in Opencode', NULL, '{"root":{"type":"root","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"For the past six months I''ve been using ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"69642d20939c257c033c3d88","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://opencode.ai","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Opencode","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":" very heavily for my own personal projects. It is an \"agentic\" CLI tool similar to Claude Code, Amp and Codex, but is (in my opinion) far more powerful and polished than every other TUI tool currently available. While most CLI tools use ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"69642db9939c257c033c3d8b","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Bubbletea","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":" or ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"69642dcc939c257c033c3d8c","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://github.com/vadimdemedes/ink","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"React Ink","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":", Opencode has created its own TUI library in Zig called ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"69642da7939c257c033c3d8a","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://github.com/anomalyco/opentui","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Opentui","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":" and it is genuinely incredible what it is capable of in terms of performance and rendering.","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Like many other tools, Opencode has the ability to ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"69642d4f939c257c033c3d89","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://opencode.ai/docs/agents/","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"define custom agents, subagents and commands","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":" with just markdown files. These are essentially prompt files, but with some config like being able to set the temperature of the response or a specific model for certain tasks (e.g. a model with a large context window like Gemini would be better used for mapping a codebase, while Opus or Sonnet would be better at planning or building a feature). Opencode comes with some default agents and subagents, like a planning agent that does not have write or edit tool ability, but is instead useful for e.g. conversing with a codebase.","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"I got the idea to try creating subagents that run in parallel recently when browsing ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"69642e5e939c257c033c3d8d","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://github.com/elithrar/dotfiles","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Matt Silverlock''s dotfiles on GitHub","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":". Here, he had his agent and command files for Opencode which specialised in parallel review subagents that are triggered by a ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"mode":"normal","text":"/review","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":1,"version":1},{"mode":"normal","text":" command:","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"\"Review the code changes using THREE (3) @code-review subagents and correlate results into a summary ranked by severity. Use the provided user guidance to steer the review and focus on specific code paths, changes, and/or areas of concern.\"","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":2,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":2},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"I loved the look of this, so I promptly copied this command and the associated subagent file to try it out. Using an open weight model like GLM 4.7, I was able to get very good feedback on some changes I was about to commit and that CodeRabbit and Copilot had missed. I imagine if I was to use Opus as my primary model for this the results would be even better. Basically, each subagent was focusing on something different, and then generated a collated summary at the end that I could go through and work through:","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"block","fields":{"id":"69643036939c257c033c3d8e","media":27,"blockName":"","blockType":"mediaBlock"},"format":"","version":2},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":" This combined approach feels to me like a good compromise between a standard, single prompt input, and the hugely expensive ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"696430ec58fa22ce54c212d6","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://venturebeat.com/technology/how-ralph-wiggum-went-from-the-simpsons-to-the-biggest-name-in-ai-right-now","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Ralph Wiggum approach","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":" that seems to be all the rage on Twitter at the moment. Potentially, if you are trying to think of different approaches to solving a problem this technique could produce something novel (or at least something you might not have thought of initially).","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0}],"direction":null}}', 'Using agents in Opencode', NULL, 'Experimenting with parallel (sub)agents in Opencode for rapid prototyping.', '2026-01-11T22:00:00.000Z', 1, 'using-agents-in-opencode', '2026-01-11T23:24:29.277Z', '2026-01-11T23:00:01.755Z', 'draft', '2026-01-11T23:00:13.307Z', '2026-01-11T23:24:29.277Z', 0, 1);
INSERT INTO "_posts_v" ("id", "parent_id", "version_title", "version_hero_image_id", "version_content", "version_meta_title", "version_meta_image_id", "version_meta_description", "version_published_at", "version_generate_slug", "version_slug", "version_updated_at", "version_created_at", "version__status", "created_at", "updated_at", "latest", "autosave") VALUES (90, 25, 'Using agents in Opencode', NULL, '{"root":{"type":"root","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"For the past six months I''ve been using ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"69642d20939c257c033c3d88","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://opencode.ai","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Opencode","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":" very heavily for my own personal projects. It is an \"agentic\" CLI tool similar to Claude Code, Amp and Codex, but is (in my opinion) far more powerful and polished than every other TUI tool currently available. While most CLI tools use ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"69642db9939c257c033c3d8b","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Bubbletea","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":" or ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"69642dcc939c257c033c3d8c","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://github.com/vadimdemedes/ink","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"React Ink","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":", Opencode has created its own TUI library in Zig called ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"69642da7939c257c033c3d8a","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://github.com/anomalyco/opentui","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Opentui","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":" and it is genuinely incredible what it is capable of in terms of performance and rendering.","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Like many other tools, Opencode has the ability to ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"69642d4f939c257c033c3d89","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://opencode.ai/docs/agents/","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"define custom agents, subagents and commands","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":" with just markdown files. These are essentially prompt files, but with some config like being able to set the temperature of the response or a specific model for certain tasks (e.g. a model with a large context window like Gemini would be better used for mapping a codebase, while Opus or Sonnet would be better at planning or building a feature). Opencode comes with some default agents and subagents, like a planning agent that does not have write or edit tool ability, but is instead useful for e.g. conversing with a codebase.","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"I got the idea to try creating subagents that run in parallel recently when browsing ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"69642e5e939c257c033c3d8d","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://github.com/elithrar/dotfiles","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Matt Silverlock''s dotfiles on GitHub","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":". Here, he had his agent and command files for Opencode which specialised in parallel review subagents that are triggered by a ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"mode":"normal","text":"/review","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":1,"version":1},{"mode":"normal","text":" command:","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"\"Review the code changes using THREE (3) @code-review subagents and correlate results into a summary ranked by severity. Use the provided user guidance to steer the review and focus on specific code paths, changes, and/or areas of concern.\"","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":2,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":2},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"I loved the look of this, so I promptly copied this command and the associated subagent file to try it out. Using an open weight model like GLM 4.7, I was able to get very good feedback on some changes I was about to commit and that CodeRabbit and Copilot had missed. I imagine if I was to use Opus as my primary model for this the results would be even better. Basically, each subagent was focusing on something different, and then generated a collated summary at the end that I could go through and work through:","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"block","fields":{"id":"69643036939c257c033c3d8e","media":27,"blockName":"","blockType":"mediaBlock"},"format":"","version":2},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"This combined approach feels to me like a good compromise between a standard, single prompt input, and the hugely expensive ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"696430ec58fa22ce54c212d6","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://venturebeat.com/technology/how-ralph-wiggum-went-from-the-simpsons-to-the-biggest-name-in-ai-right-now","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Ralph Wiggum approach","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":" that seems to be all the rage on Twitter at the moment. Potentially, if you are trying to think of different approaches to solving a problem this technique could produce something novel (or at least something you might not have thought of initially).","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"I am trying to take all of this stuff with a grain of salt still since LLMs can still make catastrophic mistakes (and doesn''t really understand what it is doing), but tools like this can definitely help to flatten the feedback loop.","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0}],"direction":null}}', 'Using agents in Opencode', NULL, 'Experimenting with parallel (sub)agents in Opencode for rapid prototyping.', '2026-01-11T22:00:00.000Z', 0, 'using-agents-in-opencode', '2026-01-11T23:25:28.237Z', '2026-01-11T23:00:01.755Z', 'published', '2026-01-11T23:25:28.264Z', '2026-01-11T23:25:28.264Z', 0, 0);
INSERT INTO "_posts_v" ("id", "parent_id", "version_title", "version_hero_image_id", "version_content", "version_meta_title", "version_meta_image_id", "version_meta_description", "version_published_at", "version_generate_slug", "version_slug", "version_updated_at", "version_created_at", "version__status", "created_at", "updated_at", "latest", "autosave") VALUES (91, 25, 'Using agents in Opencode', NULL, '{"root":{"type":"root","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"For the past six months I''ve been using ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"69642d20939c257c033c3d88","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://opencode.ai","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Opencode","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":" very heavily for my own personal projects. It is an \"agentic\" CLI tool similar to Claude Code, Amp and Codex, but is (in my opinion) far more powerful and polished than every other TUI tool currently available. While most CLI tools use ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"69642db9939c257c033c3d8b","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Bubbletea","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":" or ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"69642dcc939c257c033c3d8c","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://github.com/vadimdemedes/ink","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"React Ink","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":", Opencode has created its own TUI library in Zig called ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"69642da7939c257c033c3d8a","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://github.com/anomalyco/opentui","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Opentui","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":" and it is genuinely incredible what it is capable of in terms of performance and rendering.","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Like many other tools, Opencode has the ability to ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"69642d4f939c257c033c3d89","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://opencode.ai/docs/agents/","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"define custom agents, subagents and commands","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":" with just markdown files. These are essentially prompt files, but with some config like being able to set the temperature of the response or a specific model for certain tasks (e.g. a model with a large context window like Gemini would be better used for mapping a codebase, while Opus or Sonnet would be better at planning or building a feature). Opencode comes with some default agents and subagents, like a planning agent that does not have write or edit tool ability, but is instead useful for e.g. conversing with a codebase.","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"I got the idea to try creating subagents that run in parallel recently when browsing ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"69642e5e939c257c033c3d8d","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://github.com/elithrar/dotfiles","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Matt Silverlock''s dotfiles on GitHub","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":". Here, he had his agent and command files for Opencode which specialised in parallel review subagents that are triggered by a ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"mode":"normal","text":"/review","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":1,"version":1},{"mode":"normal","text":" command:","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"\"Review the code changes using THREE (3) @code-review subagents and correlate results into a summary ranked by severity. Use the provided user guidance to steer the review and focus on specific code paths, changes, and/or areas of concern.\"","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":2,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":2},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"I loved the look of this, so I promptly copied this command and the associated subagent file to try it out. Using an open weight model like GLM 4.7, I was able to get very good feedback on some changes I was about to commit and that CodeRabbit and Copilot had missed. I imagine if I was to use Opus as my primary model for this the results would be even better. Basically, each subagent was focusing on something different, and then generated a collated summary at the end that I could go through and work through:","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"block","fields":{"id":"69643036939c257c033c3d8e","media":27,"blockName":"","blockType":"mediaBlock"},"format":"","version":2},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"This combined approach feels to me like a good compromise between a standard, single prompt input, and the hugely expensive ","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1},{"id":"696430ec58fa22ce54c212d6","type":"link","fields":{"url":"https://venturebeat.com/technology/how-ralph-wiggum-went-from-the-simpsons-to-the-biggest-name-in-ai-right-now","newTab":true,"linkType":"custom"},"format":"","indent":0,"version":3,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"Ralph Wiggum approach","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null},{"mode":"normal","text":" that seems to be all the rage on Twitter at the moment. Potentially, if you are trying to think of different approaches to solving a problem this technique could produce something novel (or at least something you might not have thought of initially).","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0},{"type":"paragraph","format":"","indent":0,"version":1,"children":[{"mode":"normal","text":"I am trying to take all of this stuff with a grain of salt still since LLMs can still make catastrophic mistakes (and doesn''t really understand what it is doing), but tools like this can definitely help to flatten the feedback loop.","type":"text","style":"","detail":0,"format":0,"version":1}],"direction":null,"textStyle":"","textFormat":0}],"direction":null}}', 'Using agents in Opencode', NULL, 'Experimenting with parallel (sub)agents in Opencode for rapid prototyping.', '2026-01-11T22:00:00.000Z', 0, 'using-agents-in-opencode', '2026-01-11T23:29:08.172Z', '2026-01-11T23:00:01.755Z', 'published', '2026-01-11T23:29:08.186Z', '2026-01-11T23:29:08.186Z', 0, 0);
